{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nS1 [rebar:1934-63dd-4be9-4c48] lets a ticket assert `file_impact_scope: none`, and S2 [rebar:749c-7546-6fe3-4d27] makes that\nassertion buy real leverage: the attestation stops invalidating on code drift. An assertion\nthat buys leverage must be checkable, or \"declare none\" becomes the cheap way to silence the\ndrift gate. Nothing today reads the assertion for truthfulness.\n\nThe check belongs in plan review, where the plan text that would contradict the claim is\nalready in context. It must be a NARROW criterion: it fires ONLY on the presence of the\nassertion, and on every other ticket it must not run at all (no cost, no false positives).\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd one LLM criterion, id `no-file-impact`, whose entire job is: given a ticket that asserts it\nrequires no file impact, decide whether the plan the ticket actually describes requires code\nchanges.\n\n**Trigger — presence of the assertion, deterministically.** `registry.applies`\n(src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py:375) is the single proportionate-scrutiny seam, called\nfrom exactly one place (orchestrator.py:374), which already holds `ctx.state`. It gains a\n`file_impact_scope` keyword and honours a new `applies_at.require_file_impact_scope` list: a criterion\ncarrying `{\"require_file_impact_scope\": [\"none\"]}` runs only when the ticket's state says `none`.\nThis is a deterministic gate, not an LLM-routed overlay — the assertion is a field, so its\npresence is a fact, and only the CORRECTNESS judgment is left to the model. Every existing\ncriterion omits the key and is therefore unaffected.\n\n**Judgment — the LLM's.** The rubric prompt\n(`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_no_file_impact.md`, the naming convention\n`plan_review_decomp_shape.md` uses for `decomp-shape`) gets the plan text and the declared\nreason, and asks one question: does delivering THIS plan require editing any file in the\nrepository? It FAILS when the plan describes work that cannot land without an edit —\nnew/changed behavior, a new flag or config key, a test to add, a doc or generated artifact to\nupdate — and PASSES only when every deliverable lives outside the tree: an operator action in\nan external system, a coordination or decision outcome recorded on the ticket itself, a spike\nwhose product is a comment. Anti-FP guidance keeps it off the case most likely to be\nover-flagged: a ticket that merely READS or analyses code produces no edit, so analysis alone\nis not file impact. Conversely, \"docs-only\" is NOT a valid `none` reason — docs are files, and\nthe finding must say so and point at declaring those paths instead.\n\n`exec: \"1-TURN\"`, `facet: \"scope-intent\"` (alongside `necessity`), `default_posture:\n\"advisory\"`, `block_threshold: 0.95` — the repo's ship-advisory-then-dogfood-promote\nconvention (docs/plan-review-gate.md's promotion gate). It is registered in `CANONICAL_LLM`\nso the completeness guard knows it, and the generated criteria guide is regenerated so\n`rebar explain no-file-impact` works.\n\nThe completion verifier is criteria-free and is NOT touched by this story.\n\n**Structured sub-answers.** The rubric emits `assertion_plausible: yes|no|insufficient`, `reason_grounded: yes|no`, and `repo_file_change_named: yes|no`. `assertion_plausible=no` with a grounded reason produces the advisory finding; `insufficient` coaches the author to strengthen `no_file_impact_reason` but never blocks. Execution or parsing failure remains fail-open under the existing advisory criterion runtime. The deterministic trigger stays in `registry.applies` via `require_file_impact_scope=[\"none\"]`; it is not a new DET-floor check or text-regex overlay.\n\n**Reason injection.** The orchestrator reads `ctx.state.get(\"no_file_impact_reason\", \"\")` and adds it to the criterion execution context as structured field `declared_reason`; the rubric prompt renders that field alongside plan text. A prompt-render fixture asserts the persisted reason string is present in the exact LLM input, while rubric fixtures score the resulting judgment. No dedicated `PlanContext` attribute is added.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_no_file_impact.md` (new) — the rubric prompt with its\n  front-matter contract, matching the shape of an existing 1-TURN criterion prompt.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/index.json` — register the prompt.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json` — the routing entry, including\n  `applies_at.require_file_impact_scope: [\"none\"]` and the checklist.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py` — `CANONICAL_LLM` membership; `applies()` gains the\n  `file_impact_scope` keyword and the `require_file_impact_scope` condition; `_validate_routing_entry`\n  accepts and type-checks the new key.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py` — pass `file_impact_scope=ctx.state.get(\"file_impact_scope\")`\n  at the single `applies()` call site.\n- `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` — regenerated via\n  `python -m rebar.llm.plan_review.registry regenerate-criteria-guide` (never hand-edited).\n\n## Testing\n\n- `applies()` table: a criterion with `require_file_impact_scope: [\"none\"]` runs for a `none`\n  ticket and is filtered out for `undeclared` and `paths`; a criterion WITHOUT the key is\n  unaffected by any `file_impact_scope` value (pins that no existing criterion's routing changes).\n- Routing-parity gate (`registry.py`'s existing packaged-routing validator) passes with the\n  new entry, and the criteria-guide regeneration is idempotent — running it twice leaves the\n  file unchanged, and the committed file matches a fresh regeneration.\n- Prompt-contract test in the style of the existing reviewer-prompt tests: the front matter\n  parses and the criterion id resolves through `get_prompt`.\n- Three rubric fixtures exercised through the offline judge path: an operator-action plan\n  asserting `none` PASSES; a plan asserting `none` whose acceptance criteria require a new CLI\n  flag FAILS naming the contradicting plan text; a DOCS-only plan asserting `none` FAILS and\n  the finding points at declaring the doc paths.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A ticket whose `file_impact_scope` is `none` routes `no-file-impact` into the Pass-1 finder\n      set; a ticket with `paths` or `undeclared` does not (checked: `applies()`/routing test\n      asserting membership of the selected criteria list in all three states).\n- [ ] The criterion returns a FAIL finding for a plan asserting `none` that requires editing\n      any repo file (source OR docs), and PASS for a plan whose deliverables are all external\n      to the tree (checked: the three rubric fixtures).\n- [ ] `no-file-impact` is in `CANONICAL_LLM`, its routing entry passes the packaged-routing\n      parity gate, and `rebar explain no-file-impact` prints its section (checked: parity gate\n      + a CLI test).\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is a byte-exact product of the regeneration command\n      (checked: the existing regeneration-idempotence test).\n- [ ] Its posture is advisory: a FAIL from this criterion alone does not block a claim\n      (checked: a gate test asserting the verdict stays claimable).\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the full test suite pass."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785209612377262001,"uuid":"89dac9a5-846a-4909-809b-3ec06547de9f"}